WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:39.400
<v Ira Glass>Everybody knows the holidays can be stressful. And so with the holidays upon us, we thought, you know what would be nice? An hour of jokes. Let's do an hour of holiday jokes. But reflecting on this for about 10 seconds made us realize there's actually no such thing. Think about it for a second. What is your favorite Christmas joke? Right? Even kids who love Christmas and love jokes do not have Christmas jokes. To be sure this is true we actually ran a scientific test this week where we asked a group of kids to tell us their favorite Christmas joke.
<v Ira Glass>大家都知道假期可能会带来压力。随着假期临近，我们想，什么样的主意不错呢？一小时的笑话。我们来做一小时的节日笑话吧。但仅仅思考了10秒钟，我们就意识到，其实根本没有那种东西。仔细想想。你最喜欢的圣诞笑话是什么？对吧？即使是那些热爱圣诞节和喜欢笑话的孩子们，也没有圣诞笑话。为了确定这一点，我们本周进行了一项科学测试，我们要求一群孩子给我们讲他们最喜欢的圣诞笑话。

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:48.940
<v Kid 1>OK. Hmm. I don't really know one. Can they go first?
<v Kid 1>好的。嗯。我真的不知道一个。他们能先来吗？

00:00:48.940 --> 00:01:07.550
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Jonathan Menjivar, went to the playground at New York's PS 11 asking third graders for Christmas jokes. And while none of them knew any Christmas jokes, it turns out that all of them were willing on the spot-- apparently any third grader will do this-- they were willing to make up Christmas jokes.
<v Ira Glass>我们的一位制片人乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar）去了纽约PS 11小学的操场，向三年级学生索要圣诞笑话。虽然他们中没有人知道任何圣诞笑话，但事实证明，他们所有人都愿意当场——显然任何三年级学生都会这么做——他们愿意现编圣诞笑话。

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.550
<v Kid 2>Why did the reindeer cross the road?
<v Kid 2>驯鹿为什么要过马路？

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.000
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I don't know. Why?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我不知道。为什么？

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.820
<v Kid 2>To get to the horn station.
<v Kid 2>去角站。

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:27.760
<v Ira Glass>You know, the horn station.
<v Ira Glass>你知道的，角站。

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.290
<v Jonathan Menjivar>The horn? What's a horn station?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>角？什么是角站？

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.190
<v Kid 2>Where they get new horns.
<v Kid 2>他们获取新角的地方。

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.380
<v Kid 3>Oh! That makes sense sort of.
<v Kid 3>哦！那倒也说得通。

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.700
<v Kid 4>Yeah.
<v Kid 4>是的。

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:44.480
<v Ira Glass>I don't know if you could hear that. One of the kids in the background goes, "That makes sense." And everyone then agrees.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道你们能不能听到。背景里有个孩子说：“那说得通。”然后每个人都同意了。

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.260
<v Jonathan Menjivar>--sense? I'm not sure I understand.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>——说得通？我不确定我理解了。

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.800
<v Kid 2>Because reindeer have horns and then they need new horns.
<v Kid 2>因为驯鹿有角，然后他们需要新角。

00:01:51.800 --> 00:02:13.260
<v Kid 5>Yeah. And then sometimes reindeer if they get their antlers broke off, they grow back in, and in this joke, they go to a store.
<v Kid 5>是的。而且有时候驯鹿如果把鹿角撞断了，它们会重新长出来，在这个笑话里，他们去了商店。

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.510
<v Ira Glass>Here's another one.
<v Ira Glass>再来一个。

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:36.300
<v Kid 6>There was a chicken that's running around like crazy on Christmas Eve. And then Santa came. And it's like, "Hey, Santa! Can I go on?" Then Santa's like, "You can get on the deer, but not on my head!"
<v Kid 6>有一只鸡在圣诞前夜疯跑。然后圣诞老人来了。它就像说，“嘿，圣诞老人！我可以上车吗？”然后圣诞老人说，“你可以上鹿身上，但不能上我的头！”

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:43.060
<v Kid 7>Why does Santa get married with a skinny woman?
<v Kid 7>为什么圣诞老人要娶一个瘦女人？

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:55.540
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. I feel like the setup is often the best part of these jokes. Why does Santa get married to a skinny woman?
<v Ira Glass>是的。我觉得这些笑话的铺垫通常是最好的部分。为什么圣诞老人要娶一个瘦女人？

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:10.370
<v Kid 7>Because Santa feels bad that he's so fat and she's so skinny. And he wants to see somebody that's skinny.
<v Kid 7>因为圣诞老人觉得他自己太胖了，而她太瘦了，他感到很难受。他想见一个瘦的人。

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:20.750
<v Ira Glass>I'm glad to say though that some of the jokes the kids made up on the spot did actually have the structure of real jokes and worked as real jokes, with punch lines that actually made actual sense.
<v Ira Glass>不过我很高兴地说，孩子们当场编出的一些笑话确实具备了真正笑话的结构，并且作为真正的笑话发挥了作用，那些笑点确实有实际意义。

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.670
<v Kid 8>Why didn't Santa deliver the presents?
<v Kid 8>为什么圣诞老人没有派送礼物？

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.870
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I don't know.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我不知道。

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.860
<v Kid 8>Because it wasn't Christmas.
<v Kid 8>因为还没到圣诞节。

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:37.940
<v Ira Glass>OK. This next one was told to Jonathan by a little kid who first explained that they don't celebrate Christmas. They celebrate Hanukkah with the Hanukkah menorah and all that.
<v Ira Glass>好的。下一个笑话是一个小孩子告诉乔纳森的，他首先解释说他们不过圣诞节。他们庆祝光明节，有光明节灯台之类的东西。

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:41.900
<v Kid 9>What does the Christmas tree say to the menorah?
<v Kid 9>圣诞树对灯台说了什么？

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.550
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I don't know.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我不知道。

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:53.600
<v Kid 9>He says, "You don't have decorations like me. I'm more popular than you."
<v Kid 9>他说：“你没有像我这样的装饰品。我比你受欢迎。”

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:02.650
<v Ira Glass>It's funny because it's true. Jonathan wasn't sure what he should say to this little Jewish kid about that joke.
<v Ira Glass>这很有趣，因为这是真的。乔纳森不确定他该对这个犹太小孩说什么。

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.690
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Wow, the Christmas tree is mean.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>哇，这棵圣诞树真刻薄。

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:21.530
<v Kid 9>The Christmas tree is mean, but the menorah actually has good intelligence.
<v Kid 9>圣诞树很刻薄，但灯台其实很有智慧。

00:04:21.530 --> 00:05:02.060
<v Ira Glass>Good as these jokes are, fact is, if you want a Christmas holiday special that's filled with jokes, you really have to go to professionals. And so in preparation for today's show, we went to stand-up comedians and performers and we put them on stage in little clubs and venues, wherever they like to try out new material over the last few weeks. And today, from just a small club with I think 200 people in it, we bring you our Comedians of Christmas special. We have some people who you've heard of on our show before, like Mike Birbiglia, but lots of amazingly funny people who you may not know. We have actually never tried this before, and it's been completely exciting seeing what happens when we throw a theme like Christmas to these really talented, funny people.
<v Ira Glass>尽管这些笑话不错，但事实是，如果你想要一个充满笑话的圣诞节特别节目，你真的必须去找专业人士。所以为了准备今天的节目，我们找到了单口相声演员和表演者，在过去几周里，我们把他们安排在小俱乐部和各种场所的舞台上，让他们试讲新材料。今天，在一个我想大约有200人的小俱乐部里，我们为您带来了我们的“圣诞喜剧演员”特别节目。我们有一些你们以前在我们的节目中听过的人，比如迈克·比比利亚（Mike Birbiglia），但也有很多你们可能不认识的、极其有趣的人。我们其实从来没试过这样做，看到当我们把圣诞节这样的主题抛给这些非常有才华、有趣的人时会发生什么，这真是太令人兴奋了。

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:26.720
<v Ira Glass>And so, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>所以，来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:36.850
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Christ Has Prison.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，基督在监狱。

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:46.220
<v Ira Glass>We were worried that was a little too Eastery. It's a Christmas show.
<v Ira Glass>我们担心那太像复活节了。这是一个圣诞节目。

00:05:46.220 --> 00:06:08.560
<v Ira Glass>Wyatt Cenac is a correspondent and a performer for The Daily Show with Jon Stewart . And he has this story about spreading Christmas cheer in Texas. Please welcome him.
<v Ira Glass>怀亚特·塞纳克（Wyatt Cenac）是《乔恩·斯图尔特每日秀》的通讯记者和表演者。他有一个关于在德克萨斯州传播圣诞欢乐的故事。请欢迎他。

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.740
<v Wyatt Cenac>Thank you. I am from Texas.
<v Wyatt Cenac>谢谢。我来自德克萨斯州。

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.600
<v Wyatt Cenac>[LAUGHTER]
<v Wyatt Cenac>（笑声）

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:37.060
<v Ira Glass>That's not normally the response I expect. Its very weird because when you tell somebody that you're from Texas, their response is, "Oh my God, I love Austin!" Which is cool, but I grew up in Dallas. And people will be like, "Oh, I'm sorry. Austin's pretty awesome. Austin's great."
<v Ira Glass>这不是我通常预期的反应。这很奇怪，因为当你告诉别人你来自德克萨斯州时，他们的反应是，“哦，天哪，我爱奥斯汀！”这很酷，但我是在达拉斯长大的。人们会说，“哦，抱歉。奥斯汀挺棒的。奥斯汀很好。”

00:06:37.060 --> 00:07:12.420
<v Ira Glass>It's weird. I've lived here in New York now for about two and a half years, and I was actually born here. I lived here as a baby. And it's nice to be back. The city has changed a lot since I lived here as a baby. When I was a baby, everywhere you went it was just colors and shapes everywhere. Gentrification has changed all of that. In my old neighborhood, I remember on the corner there was this amorphous yellow blob. Now it's a Chipotle.
<v Ira Glass>这很奇怪。我现在在纽约住了大约两年半了，其实我出生在这里。我小时候住过这里。回来感觉很好。自从我小时候住在这里以来，这座城市发生了很大变化。当我还是个婴儿时，你所到之处到处都是色彩和形状。绅士化改变了这一切。在我原来的街区，我记得拐角处有一团无定形的黄色东西。现在是一家Chipotle快餐店。

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:46.250
<v Ira Glass>When my mother hit her 50s, she went through a bit of a midlife crisis. And typically, a midlife crisis is something that people associate with men. Men who are struggling with getting older, they maybe go out and buy themselves a Corvette or some other type of penis car. And my mother didn't do that. What she did for her midlife crisis was she went to church wearing those big fancy church lady hats that star in Tyler Perry movies.
<v Ira Glass>当我母亲步入50岁时，她经历了一点中年危机。通常，中年危机是人们与男性联系在一起的东西。那些在衰老中挣扎的男人，他们可能会出去给自己买一辆科尔维特或其他什么类型的“阴茎车”。但我母亲没这样做。她中年危机所做的是，她戴着泰勒·派瑞（Tyler Perry）电影里那种抢眼的教堂女士帽子去教堂。

00:07:46.250 --> 00:08:12.740
<v Ira Glass>You see, a man in a midlife crisis maybe goes and gets himself an old Mustang and soups it up. And my mother did the same thing. She would take these hats, and you'd see her at church on Sunday with a bunch of other middle aged ladies. And they'd have tricked these hats out with lace and ribbons and flowers and dodo eggs, whatever they could get their hands on. They'd put rims and a spoiler on there if they could. And this is what my mother did.
<v Ira Glass>你看，中年危机的男人可能会给自己弄一辆旧野马并改装一下。我母亲也做了同样的事。她会拿这些帽子，你会在周日看到她和一群中年女士在教堂里。她们会用蕾丝、丝带、花朵和渡渡鸟蛋，任何她们能弄到的东西把这些帽子装饰起来。如果可以的话，她们会在上面装上轮毂和扰流板。这就是我母亲所做的。

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:37.090
<v Ira Glass>And in the same way that when a man goes through a midlife crisis, he might find himself a trophy girlfriend, some 20-year-old who will spike up his hair and put him in ill-fitting T-shirts that have sparkly dragon skeletons on them. My mother did the same thing. She went to church and she found herself a trophy boyfriend named Jesus Christ.
<v Ira Glass>就像男人经历中年危机时可能会找一个奖杯女友一样，某个20岁的女孩会把他的头发弄得竖起来，让他穿上印有闪亮龙骨架的不合身T恤。我母亲也做了同样的事。她去了教堂，给自己找了一个叫耶稣基督的奖杯男友。

00:08:37.090 --> 00:09:18.680
<v Ira Glass>And when you think about it, Jesus is really the best trophy boyfriend for a menopausal woman. He's older, but he doesn't show it. He doesn't care if you feel like you're aging and you've got crows feet or you feel bloated or anything like that. He doesn't care. He loves unconditionally. If you want to spend all day in aerobic slippers, guess what? So does he. That's his uniform. Oh, and the most important one. He's made of bread and wine. He's perfect.
<v Ira Glass>仔细想想，耶稣确实是更年期女性最好的奖杯男友。他年纪大了，但看不出来。他不在乎你是否觉得自己老了，是否有鱼尾纹，或者是否感到臃肿之类的。他不在乎。他无条件地爱。如果你想整天穿着健美拖鞋，猜猜怎么着？他也一样。那是他的制服。哦，最重要的一点。他是用面包和酒做的。他是完美的。

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:34.660
<v Ira Glass>And around the same time that my mother started seeing Jesus, the church decided that this Christmas they were going to do some Christmas prison ministry. And what they were going to do is they were going to take Christmas gifts to prison inmates. And my mother loved this idea, so she signed up.
<v Ira Glass>就在我母亲开始见耶稣的同时，教会决定这个圣诞节他们要做一些圣诞监狱事工。他们要做的是带圣诞礼物给监狱囚犯。我母亲很喜欢这个主意，所以她报名了。

00:09:34.660 --> 00:10:34.050
<v Ira Glass>And you might be asking yourself, What kind of Christmas gift do you give to a prison inmate? I mean, their list is probably pretty simple, it's probably just freedom and sex, not necessarily in that order. The church couldn't deliver on those things, so they thought the next best thing would be a bag of fruit. And what it was, it was a paper bag that had been decorated by the children of the church. They drew little pictures on it. And then inside that bag was an apple and an orange and a little prayer card. Because it's church, nothing's free. And then on the flip side of that card was a calendar, which really seems like the worst thing you could give a prison inmate as a gift. That's the Christmas prison equivalent of some kid waking up on Christmas morning to find a pair of socks when he has no feet. But at the same time, it's the thought that counts.
<v Ira Glass>你可能会问自己，你会给监狱囚犯什么样的圣诞礼物？我的意思是，他们的名单可能很简单，可能只是自由和性，不一定是这个顺序。教会无法提供这些，所以他们认为下一个最好的东西是一袋水果。那是一个由教会孩子们装饰过的纸袋。他们在上面画了一些小图画。袋子里有一个苹果、一个橘子和一张小祈祷卡。因为是教会，没什么是免费的。那张卡片的另一面是一个日历，这看起来真的是你能给监狱囚犯的最糟糕的礼物。这相当于某个孩子在圣诞节早晨醒来发现一双袜子，但他却没有脚。但同时，心意最重要。

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:53.060
<v Ira Glass>And my mother was doing this thing. It was very noble. Not everybody would go and give Christmas to prison inmates. I thought, wow, that's really awesome of her that she wants to do that. I'm going to do something nice for her. So that morning, I figured I was going to wake up and I was going to make her some pancakes because she's got a big day ahead of her.
<v Ira Glass>我母亲在做这件事。这非常高尚。并不是每个人都会去给监狱囚犯送去圣诞礼物。我想，哇，她想这样做真的很了不起。我要为她做点好事。所以那天早上，我想我要醒来给她做些薄煎饼，因为她前面有忙碌的一天。

00:10:53.060 --> 00:11:27.070
<v Ira Glass>And that's when I found out that my mother would not be going to the maximum security prison. My mother had never had any intention of going. See, she liked the idea of doing a Christian mission, but she also liked the idea of spending the day in bed watching Wheel of Fortune. And since she couldn't do both, she decided she would send me to prison in her place. It was at that moment of my life that I understood what outsourcing was.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时我发现我母亲根本不会去最高安全级别的监狱。我母亲从来没打算去。你看，她喜欢做基督教传教的想法，但她也喜欢整天躺在床上看《命运之轮》电视节目。既然她不能两者兼顾，她决定派我去监狱代替她。就是在人生的那一刻，我明白了什么是外包。

00:11:27.070 --> 00:12:04.170
<v Ira Glass>My mother decided to send me, my little brother, and my stepfather, who up to this point had been OK with the open relationship that my mother, he, and Jesus had been having. And we get out to the prison. And if you've never been to prison, the first thing that happens is you take off your Santa cap, because you feel silly. It doesn't seem right. And it's like everything you've seen in movies. There's a big buzzer that's like, errr, and some guy's like, "Come on through!" And you do that and then there's a caclunk of the door and all that stuff. And we do all that, and we're inside the prison.
<v Ira Glass>我母亲决定派我、我弟弟和我继父去，直到现在我继父对我母亲、他和耶稣之间正在进行的这种开放式关系还觉得可以接受。我们到了监狱。如果你从没去过监狱，第一件事就是你要摘下你的圣诞帽，因为你会觉得很傻。这看起来不太对劲。就像你在电影里看到的一切。有一个大蜂鸣器发出“呃……”的声音，有个家伙说，“进来吧！”你就那么做了，然后门发出“咔哒”一声，诸如此类。我们做了所有这些，我们就在监狱里面了。

00:12:04.170 --> 00:13:02.260
<v Ira Glass>But what doesn't happen most times in the movies or an episode of NBC's Lockup is because there was a thunderstorm going on, there was like big flash of lightning and then this really loud thunder crack. And then the power went out. Yeah. It was just darkness in prison. And at the time, the movie Jurassic Park was big. And all I can think to myself is, [BLEEP], the raptors are loose and all I have are these apples to defend myself. The oranges I'll keep to fend off scurvy. But the apples. And then eventually the power comes back on, and everybody's fine. I have to pick up some of the warning apples that I threw out.
<v Ira Glass>但在电影或NBC电视台《锁闭》节目中通常不会发生的是，因为当时正在打雷，有一道巨大的闪电，然后是巨大的雷声。然后电源断了。是的。监狱里一片黑暗。当时电影《侏罗纪公园》很火。我脑子里能想到的就是，“操，迅猛龙跑出来了，而我手里只有这些苹果来保护自己。”橘子我留着防坏血病。但苹果。然后最终电源恢复了，每个人都没事。我得捡起我扔掉的一些警告苹果。

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:16.690
<v Ira Glass>And we're doing this. We're giving Christmas to these guys. And in prison they tend to separate the prisoners and categorize them in different ways. And there are the guys who are in solitary confinement. It's called the hole. They're considered the worst of the worst.
<v Ira Glass>我们就在做这件事。我们正在给这些人送去圣诞节。在监狱里，他们倾向于把囚犯分开并以不同的方式分类。有些人是被单独监禁的。这叫“小黑屋”。他们被认为是最坏中的最坏。

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:48.260
<v Ira Glass>And then on the other side of the spectrum, they have inmates called trustees. And these are inmates who have decided they want to rehabilitate themselves. They may never make parole, but they've decided that they want to be better people now than they were when they walked in. And so they take counseling, they take classes, they get jobs, and they're given more responsibilities. And they don't live in cells, they live in barracks with bunks. And so these are the people we're going to be giving Christmas gifts to.
<v Ira Glass>然后在光谱的另一端，他们有被称为“受托人”的囚犯。这些是决定想要改造自己的囚犯。他们可能永远无法假释，但他们决定现在要成为比刚进来时更好的人。所以他们参加咨询，参加课程，找工作，他们被赋予了更多责任。他们不住牢房，他们住在有双层床的营房里。所以这些人就是我们要送去圣诞礼物的人。

00:13:48.260 --> 00:14:40.780
<v Ira Glass>And we go there and we go into the barracks and we start handing these gifts out, and it's amazing watching these guys get these gifts. Their faces just light up. Just the bag itself, the drawings on the bags. You would've assumed that those were drawn specifically for them because they're so proud of these drawings. They are like, "Yo, check it out. This kid drew me a car, and it's red, and that's my favorite color. It's awesome." Another inmate would be like, "Check it out. This kid drew me a house, and then there's a pool. And that's the house I want to live in." Another inmate was like, "Check it out. This kid drew me a horse with a spear sticking out of his head." "That's a unicorn." "It's a spear horse!"
<v Ira Glass>我们去了那里，进入营房，开始分发这些礼物，看着这些人收到礼物真是太棒了。他们的脸亮了起来。单单是袋子本身，袋子上的画。你会以为那些是专门为他们画的，因为他们对这些画如此自豪。他们说，“哟，看看这个。这个孩子给我画了一辆车，是红色的，那是我最喜欢的颜色。太棒了。”另一个囚犯会说，“看看这个。这个孩子给我画了一所房子，还有一个游泳池。那是我想要住的房子。”另一个囚犯说，“看看这个。这个孩子给我画了一匹头上插着长矛的马。”“那是独角兽。”“那是长矛马！”

00:14:40.780 --> 00:15:24.140
<v Ira Glass>And to see how happy they were getting these gifts. And for this moment, they're not inmates in a prison anymore. As they're going through these gifts, they're just kids on Christmas. And they're so happy. And they're emptying out the bags and just smoothing out the bags and hanging them on their bunks, and you're watching just the sheer pleasure on their faces as they're going through the gifts. And you're watching that fade away as they find the calendar. "Like seriously, man, what am I supposed to do with this?" Beyond that, they were very appreciative. They were appreciative for the apple, appreciative for the orange, appreciative for the bag. The calendar not so much.
<v Ira Glass>看到他们收到这些礼物有多高兴。在这一刻，他们不再是监狱里的囚犯了。当他们翻看这些礼物时，他们只是圣诞节的孩子。他们太高兴了。他们倒空袋子，把袋子铺平，挂在床铺上，你看着他们翻看礼物时脸上的纯粹快乐。然后你看着当你发现日历时那种快乐消失了。“说真的，伙计，我该拿这个怎么办？”除此之外，他们非常感激。他们感激苹果，感激橘子，感激袋子。日历就没那么感激了。

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:55.410
<v Ira Glass>But we give everybody the gifts, and I notice there's still a lot of gifts left. And so the guard who had been taking us around was like, "You know what? Let's take those and give those to the guys in solitary." Really? Does Santa's naughty list mean nothing at this point? I mean, what happened to be good for goodness' sake? Better not pout, better not cry, better not shout? Santa has problems with those, but murder he's cool with? Really?
<v Ira Glass>但我们把礼物发给了所有人，我注意到还有很多礼物剩下。所以带我们参观的守卫说，“你知道吗？把那些拿去给单独监禁的人吧。”真的吗？圣诞老人的“淘气名单”在这一点上毫无意义吗？我的意思是，那个“好孩子”的初衷发生了什么？“最好别噘嘴，最好别哭，最好别大叫”？圣诞老人对这些有意见，但谋杀他就觉得可以？真的吗？

00:15:55.410 --> 00:16:52.660
<v Ira Glass>And we go to solitary. Solitary, each of the doors are these metal doors. And there are these metal doors. And then on the door is a smaller little over door about the size of a shoebox. And that's there. And then there's a guard, and he's got a stick. And he'll take the stick. He takes it to the little shoebox sized oven door and opens up and he's like, "All right, give me a gift." "All right. Do I get a stick?" "No, you'll be fine." "Really? Because you have a stick." "Do it." So I start giving the gifts, and I'm just throwing them in the hole. And we go to the next one, and it's like I'm playing Quidditch, just tossing them in.
<v Ira Glass>我们去了单独监禁区。单独监禁区，每扇门都是这种金属门。有这些金属门。门上有一个小小的、大约鞋盒大小的过门。就是那样。然后有一个守卫，他有一根棍子。他拿着棍子。他把它伸到鞋盒大小的烤箱门那里，打开它，他说，“好吧，给我一个礼物。”“好吧。我能得到一根棍子吗？”“不，你会没事的。”“真的吗？因为你有棍子。”“做吧。”所以我开始分发礼物，我只是把它们扔进洞里。我们去下一个，就像我在玩魁地奇，只是把它扔进去。

00:16:52.660 --> 00:17:23.430
<v Ira Glass>And eventually we get to this one, and I throw the gift in, and the guard is about to stick up the door again, and from behind the door I hear this voice and he goes, "Wait. Hold on a second. Hold on. I want to shake his hand." "Well, my friend, I'd love to, but I'm sure the guard has something to say about that." "No, it's fine. Go ahead. Do it." "Really?"
<v Ira Glass>最终我们到了这个，我把礼物扔进去，守卫正准备再次封住门，我从门后听到一个声音，他说，“等等。等等。等等。我想握个手。”“好吧，朋友，我很想，但我肯定守卫对此会有话说。”“不，没关系。去做吧。去做吧。”“真的吗？”

00:17:23.430 --> 00:18:02.960
<v Ira Glass>And I'm terrified, because now I can't see anything except eyes back there. And I'm slowly making my way and just thinking, I could shake his hand, and he could eat my fingers. And I'm nervous. And I'm walking closer, and I still can't see anything. And then all of a sudden this hand just comes out. It's a big, meaty hand, because it's a prison hand. And I walk over, and my hand sort of nervously grabs his and shakes it. And he holds on to it and pulls me a little closer.
<v Ira Glass>我吓坏了，因为现在我除了后面的眼睛什么也看不见。我慢慢地走过去，心想，我可以握他的手，他可以把我的手指吃掉。我很紧张。我走得更近了，但我还是什么也看不见。突然一只手伸了出来。那是一只粗壮的手，因为那是监狱的手。我走过去，我的手有点紧张地抓住他的并摇了摇。他抓住我不放，把我拉得更近了一点。

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:47.450
<v Ira Glass>He's like, "Yeah, I thought I knew you. Yeah, I know you." I'm like, "Oh, no you don't. No. No you don't." And he's convinced. He's like, "Yeah, I know you. I know you, man." And now I'm starting to think to myself, Does he know me? He's really convinced. "Yeah, I thought it was you. Yeah, I know you. You're from Austin." Which at that particular moment, I had never felt more pride to be from Dallas. I was just like, "No, I'm from Dallas, [BLEEP]!" At which point, he was like, "Oh, you should really tell people you're from Austin. It's a much nicer place." Thank you, everybody. Thank you.
<v Ira Glass>他说，“是的，我想我认识你。是的，我认识你。”我说，“哦，不，你不认识。不。不，你不认识。”他很确信。他说，“是的，我认识你。我认识你，伙计。”现在我开始心想，他认识我吗？他真的非常确信。“是的，我以为是你。是的，我认识你。你来自奥斯汀。”在那一刻，我从来没有因为来自达拉斯而感到如此自豪。我只是说，“不，我来自达拉斯，[消音]！”此时，他说，“哦，你真的应该告诉别人你来自奥斯汀。那是一个好得多的地方。”谢谢大家。谢谢。

