00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:29.210
<v Ira Glass>Despite our nation's best efforts to turn Christmas into a festival that's about gifts and toys and buying stuff, it is still a religious holiday. Please welcome comedian Mike Birbiglia.
<v Ira Glass>尽管我们国家尽最大努力把圣诞节变成一个关于礼物、玩具和买东西的节日，但它仍然是一个宗教节日。请欢迎喜剧演员迈克·比比利亚。

00:24:29.210 --> 00:25:27.790
<v Mike Birbiglia>One of the reasons Christmas was a really big deal to me when I was a kid was that I was really good at being Catholic. And not everyone was. My brother Joe only came to church on Christmas and even then he'd show up late. He'd be like, "Mike, the later you show up, the shorter it is." And then he'd change the lyrics to the hymns. There was this one we used to sing every week that goes, "Christ has died. Christ is risen. Christ will come again." And Joe's lyrics were, "Christ has lied. Christ is in prison. Christ will come at 10:00." I'd try so hard not to laugh, because I was on Jesus's team.
<v Mike Birbiglia>圣诞节对我小时候来说之所以是一件大事，原因之一是我很擅长做天主教徒。并不是每个人都这样。我弟弟乔（Joe）只在圣诞节来教堂，即使那样他也会迟到。他会说，“迈克，你来得越晚，它就越短。”然后他会改写赞美诗的歌词。我们过去每周都会唱一首，“基督已死。基督已复活。基督必将来临。”乔的歌词是，“基督撒谎了。基督在监狱里。基督将在10点来。”我会努力不笑，因为我是耶稣团队的。

00:25:27.790 --> 00:26:11.720
<v Mike Birbiglia>Well I went to church every week with my mom and I thought it made me her favorite. My mom decided early on that I would be a priest. And I really thought that I would, and I liked the idea. So every week we'd go to church. When other kids would go to the park or the mall, we'd go to church. And sometimes I'd be like, "Hey, Mom, I want to go to the mall." And my mom would say, "Like fun are you going to the mall." That's an expression that my mom uses. Like fun. Which is her way of saying, Like Hell, because she's Catholic. And for Catholics, Hell is fun.
<v Mike Birbiglia>嗯，我每周都和我妈去教堂，我觉得这让我成了她最喜欢的孩子。我妈很早就决定我会成为一名牧师。我确实想过我会成为牧师，我也喜欢这个想法。所以每周我们都会去教堂。当其他孩子去公园或商场时，我们去教堂。有时我会说，“嘿，妈，我想去商场。”我妈会说，“你就像去商场一样去玩吧。”那是我妈用的一种表达方式。就像玩。这是她说“该死”的方式，因为她是天主教徒。对于天主教徒来说，地狱才是好玩的。

00:26:11.720 --> 00:27:11.180
<v Mike Birbiglia>When you're seven, and your mom sends you to Catholic school, it's all pretty simple. They're like, "There's this guy Jesus, and he totally loves you." And you're like, "Great." And they're like, "You love him too, right?" And you're like, "I'm sorry. Do I know this guy?" And they're like, "You know, from the cross. That guy loves you, and you love him." And it starts innocently enough, as innocently as man/boy love can start. You just kind of accept it. "There's this guy, Jesus, who everybody's afraid of but everybody loves because he loves everybody. And a long time ago, some people killed him, and it's not totally your fault. So don't be scared or sad, because he's living forever next to God who's his dad, even though he's also God. And also there's this whole Holy Spirit part too that no one really understands. Am I going too fast for you, seven-year-old boy?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>当你七岁时，你妈送你去天主教学校，一切都很简单。他们说，“有这个叫耶稣的家伙，他完全爱你。”你说，“太棒了。”他们说，“你也爱他，对吧？”你说，“抱歉。我认识这个家伙吗？”他们说，“你知道的，从十字架上。那个家伙爱你，你也爱他。”这开始得足够天真，像人/男孩之爱能开始的那样天真。你只是接受了它。“有这个叫耶稣的家伙，每个人都怕他，但每个人都爱他，因为他爱每个人。很久以前，有些人杀了他，这不完全是你的错。所以不要害怕或难过，因为他正住在上帝旁边，上帝是他爸爸，尽管他也是上帝。而且还有这整个圣灵的部分，没人真正理解。我刚才说得太快了吗，七岁的小男孩？”

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:47.230
<v Mike Birbiglia>So you go with that for a few years. And then you're 11 and you get the word that Father Grady wants you to be one of his new altar boys. He's seen your work at recess on the kickball field, and he thinks you've got what it takes to snuff out candles and hold a chalice and not trip and fall on your robe. So I became an altar boy. And the answer's no. I wasn't, and I think it's because they knew I was a talker. I have that look about me.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以你接受那个几年。然后你11岁了，你听到消息说格雷迪神父希望你成为他的新祭坛男孩。他看到了你在踢球场上课间休息时的表现，他认为你有能力熄灭蜡烛、拿着圣餐杯，而不会绊倒在长袍上。所以我成了祭坛男孩。答案是不。我不是，我想这是因为他们知道我是个话痨。我有那种外表。

00:27:47.230 --> 00:28:22.130
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I loved being an altar boy because church was glamorous. The priests have these multicolor robes. As an altar boy, even you have a robe, just a plain white one. It's like karate where they start you with the white belt and then you get the different color belts once you start kicking some Devil ass. So I loved being an altar boy, especially on Christmas. That's when I could really show off, because Christmas was cool. I mean everyone came to Christmas Mass. People who you never saw at church came to Christmas mass, people like my brother Joe.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我喜欢做祭坛男孩，因为教堂很迷人。牧师有这些多色长袍。作为祭坛男孩，即使你也有长袍，只是纯白色的。就像空手道，他们让你从白带开始，然后当你开始踢恶魔屁股时，你会得到不同颜色的腰带。所以我喜欢做祭坛男孩，特别是在圣诞节。那是当我可以真正炫耀的时候，因为圣诞节很酷。我的意思是每个人都来参加圣诞弥撒。你从未在教堂见过的人都来参加圣诞弥撒，比如我弟弟乔。

00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:54.870
<v Mike Birbiglia>And they'd see me there with their families and they'd see me in my robe, all fitting in around the church. I acted like I owned the place. I'd go, "Oh yeah? You want to walk up on the altar? That's probably not a great idea. That's where J-Dog hangs out." "You know Jesus too? Yeah, he's a pretty good friend of mine. It's cool of you to come by, but seriously where were you during Advent?" That's for the real diehard Catholics, that last one.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他们会看到我在那里和他们的家人在一起，他们会看到我穿着长袍，在教堂周围合群。我表现得好像我拥有这个地方一样。我会说，“哦是吗？你想走到祭坛上？那可能不是个好主意。那是J-Dog（耶稣）待的地方。”“你也认识耶稣吗？是的，他是我的好朋友。你来这里很酷，但说真的，你在将临期去哪了？”那是给真正的死忠天主教徒的，最后一句。

00:28:54.870 --> 00:29:28.420
<v Mike Birbiglia>Shortly after I finished college, my mom developed this condition where as far as the doctors could tell, part of her spine was pushing into her spinal cord. And they weren't sure what was happening, but she was experiencing chronic pain all over her body. And so the doctors did this operation, and the problem is the pain didn't go away. So my mom was faced with both the original pain and then the pain of this operation. And she was laid up for months. She was in bed. And so I came home and lived with them to administer her pain meds, and get her meals.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我大学毕业后不久，我妈患上了这种病，据医生所知，她脊椎的一部分压迫到了她的脊髓。他们不确定发生了什么，但她全身都在经历慢性疼痛。所以医生做了这个手术，问题是疼痛没有消失。所以我妈既面临着最初的疼痛，又面临着手术的疼痛。她躺了好几个月。她卧床不起。所以我回家和他们住在一起，给她吃止痛药，给她做饭。

00:29:28.420 --> 00:30:09.950
<v Mike Birbiglia>She was also prescribed this drug called Ativan, which is an anti-anxiety. And she was supposed to take it three times a day, but pretty soon she wanted to take it five or six times a day. And I wasn't sure why. And I asked her, and she pulled me aside and she said, "I'm dying." And I was so upset that I called her doctor and I asked him if my mom was dying. And he said, "Well we don't know what's happening exactly, but there's no reason to believe she's dying. Just make sure she doesn't take too much Ativan. It's making her delirious."
<v Mike Birbiglia>她还被开了这种叫阿提凡（Ativan）的药，是一种抗焦虑药。她本应该一天吃三次，但很快她想一天吃五六次。我不确定为什么。我问她，她把我拉到一边说，“我要死了。”我太难过了，我打电话给她医生，问他我妈是不是快死了。他说，“嗯，我们不知道确切发生了什么，但没有理由相信她快死了。确保她不要吃太多阿提凡。这让她产生了谵妄。”

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:47.650
<v Mike Birbiglia>So I hid the Ativan. And it drove my mother crazy. At one point she shouted, "Michael, where is the Ativan?" And I said, "Mom, I can't give you the Ativan." And she said, "I am your mother and if I say get me the Ativan, you get me the Ativan." And I said, "Mom, like fun am I getting you the Ativan."
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以我藏起了阿提凡。这让我母亲快疯了。有一次她大喊，“迈克尔，阿提凡在哪里？”我说，“妈，我不能给你阿提凡。”她说，“我是你妈，如果我说给我阿提凡，你就得给我阿提凡。”我说，“妈，我绝不可能给你阿提凡。”

00:30:47.650 --> 00:31:36.490
<v Mike Birbiglia>She started pulling me aside every day and she'd say, "Michael, I'm dying and I'm going to Hell." And the way she said it wasn't in that exaggerated way that people use it, where they say, I'm going to Hell. She said it as though it was the next stop on her train, like it was this actual location. It wasn't a concept. It was like, I'm going to Hell on Tuesday. And I'd say, "Mom, you're not going to Hell." And she said, "No, there's things that I've done in my life that you don't know about." And I'd say "Mom, if you're going to Hell, Hell is going to be really crowded, because I had you as a first-round draft pick for Heaven."
<v Mike Birbiglia>她开始每天把我拉到一边，说，“迈克尔，我要死了，我要下地狱了。”她说这话的方式不像人们夸张地说“我要下地狱了”那样。她说的好像那是她火车的下一站，就像那是一个实际的位置。这不是一个概念。就像，“我星期二要下地狱了。”我说，“妈，你不会下地狱的。”她说，“不，我一生中做过一些你不知道的事情。”我说，“妈，如果你要下地狱，地狱会非常拥挤，因为我把你作为上天堂的第一轮选秀首选。”

00:31:36.490 --> 00:32:15.320
<v Mike Birbiglia>And she wouldn't laugh. I mean there wasn't a lot of laughing in this period. And eventually my mom got better. But the whole thing shook me up. It was this side of Catholicism I never thought about as a kid. And I never went back to church. I can't be part of an organization that convinced my mom she was headed for eternal torture. Like Hell am I going to a church like that, except of course on Christmas.
<v Mike Birbiglia>她没有笑。我的意思是这段时间没多少笑声。最终我妈好转了。但整件事让我感到震惊。这是我小时候从未想过的天主教的一面。我再也没回过教堂。我不能成为一个让我想起我妈正走向永恒折磨的组织的一部分。我绝不会去那样的教堂，当然圣诞节除外。

