00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:44.540
<v Jenny Slate>Well I didn't ask for any presents this year, because I have everything that I need. And I would like to say that the best gift that I could have ever received is to have a wonderful boyfriend like Gabe Liedman.
<v Jenny Slate>嗯，我今年没要任何礼物，因为我拥有我需要的一切。我想说的是，我能收到的最好的礼物就是拥有一个像盖布·里德曼这样出色的男朋友。

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:47.170
<v Gabe Liedman>Or whatever that you have.
<v Gabe Liedman>或者你有的任何什么。

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:49.450
<v Jenny Slate>That's right. So, Season's Greetings.
<v Jenny Slate>没错。所以，节日问候。

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:50.070
<v Gabe Liedman>Season's Greetings, guys.
<v Gabe Liedman>节日问候，伙计们。

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:51.610
<v Jenny Slate>Let's find some mistletoe!
<v Jenny Slate>我们去找点槲寄生吧！

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:55.670
<v Gabe Liedman>Yeah, and take it down so no one gets hurt! It's poisonous.
<v Gabe Liedman>是的，把它取下来，这样就不会有人受伤了！它有毒。

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:57.370
<v Jenny Slate>Happy Holidays! Thank you.
<v Jenny Slate>节日快乐！谢谢。

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:04.540
<v Gabe Liedman>Goodnight.
<v Gabe Liedman>晚安。

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:17.440
<v Ira Glass>Jenny Slate wrote and voiced the award-winning film Marcel the Shell with Shoes On , and can be seen on HBO's Bored to Death . Gabe Liedman writes for videogum.com, and can be seen on AOL.com's new series, The One.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮·斯莱特编剧并配音了获奖影片《穿着鞋子的贝壳玛塞尔》，可以在HBO的《凡人烦恼》中看到。盖布·里德曼为videogum.com写稿，可以在AOL.com的新系列《唯一》中看到。

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:21.360
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, 'Twas the Fight Before Christmas.
<v Ira Glass>第五幕，“圣诞前的战斗”。

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:49.740
<v Ira Glass>So we booked a bunch of comedians to do stories about Christmas. And for reasons I don't even understand or care to speculate about just a number of them ended up doing stories that heavily involved their moms. Go figure. Please welcome our next comedian, Julian McCullough.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们预订了一堆喜剧演员来做关于圣诞节的故事。由于一些我甚至不理解或不想猜测的原因，他们中的不少人最终做了严重涉及他们母亲的故事。想一想。请欢迎我们的下一位喜剧演员，朱利安·麦卡洛（Julian McCullough）。

00:37:49.740 --> 00:38:55.230
<v Julian Mccullough>When I was a 14 years old, I lived with my dad and my mom in southern New Jersey. And one Sunday, my mom was like, "Hey, let's go out to your favorite restaurant for breakfast." And I was like, "Why? OK." So we go to breakfast and after we leave we're driving home, and she stops. The car has to stop at a railroad crossing. And she turns to me and she says, "How would you feel if I moved to Tennessee?" And I kind of understood the finality of what she was saying, even though she didn't use the word divorce or anything like that. And it's amazing how fast defense mechanisms can kick in, because I remember immediately my reaction was, "Oh my God. Go. When was the last time Patricia did something for Patricia?"
<v Julian Mccullough>当我14岁时，我和我的父亲和母亲住在新泽西州南部。一个周日，我妈说，“嘿，我们去你最喜欢的餐厅吃早餐吧。”我说，“为什么？好吧。”所以我们去吃早餐，离开后我们在开车回家，她停了下来。车必须在一个铁路道口停下。她转过身对我说，“如果我搬到田纳西州，你会怎么想？”我有点理解她所说的话的最终性，尽管她没有用离婚这个词或者类似的词。令人惊奇的是防御机制能激发得有多快，因为我记得我的直接反应是，“哦，天哪。去吧。帕特里夏（Patricia）上次为帕特里夏做点什么是什么时候？”

00:38:55.230 --> 00:39:43.320
<v Julian Mccullough>So another thing you should know is that this didn't seem that strange to me, because my parents had moved us so many times. I was born in Philadelphia. We had lived in Portland, Oakland, San Francisco, Pittsfield, Massachusetts, and southern New Jersey. People always asked if I was an Army brat, and it wasn't true at all. My parents would just get bored and move us. So I was used to an unstable existence. I was new in school almost every year. And I just thought this seemed kind of logical for my mom to all of a sudden move to Tennessee for no reason.
<v Julian Mccullough>你应该知道的另一件事是，这对我来说看起来并不奇怪，因为我的父母搬家搬了太多次了。我出生在费城。我们住过波特兰、奥克兰、旧金山、马萨诸塞州皮茨菲尔德和新泽西州南部。人们总是问我是不是军人家属，根本不是。我父母只是会感到无聊，然后就搬走。所以我习惯了不稳定的存在。我几乎每年在学校都是新生。我只是觉得我妈突然搬到田纳西州去没有任何理由似乎很合乎逻辑。

00:39:43.320 --> 00:40:56.410
<v Julian Mccullough>So that started a series of visits to my mom down in Tennessee for the next five years. I would go in the summers when I didn't have school. And I really started to dread it. By the second time that I went to visit her, my mom's new boyfriend, Rick, showed up at the airport to pick me up. And Rick showed up on a Harley, which is a motorcycle, to pick someone up at an airport. And I was like, "That's awesome. I'll just leave my bags on the twirly thing until I go back to New Jersey." He was wearing a T-shirt that's very popular. It's a Harley Davidson T-shirt, and the back of it said, "If you can read this, the bitch fell off." And I was like, "That's my mom. Is she OK? Let's get her."
<v Julian Mccullough>所以这开始了接下来的五年里我到田纳西州看望我母亲的一系列探访。我会在我没有学校的夏天去。我真的开始害怕了。第二次去探望她时，我母亲的新男友里克（Rick）出现在机场接我。里克出现在哈雷摩托车上，这是一种摩托车，去机场接人。我说，“太棒了。我就把我的包留在转盘上直到我回新泽西州。”他穿了一件很受欢迎的T恤。那是一件哈雷戴维森T恤，背部写着，“如果你能读懂这个，那个婊子掉下来了。”我说，“那是我的母亲。她没事吧？让我们去救她。”

00:40:56.410 --> 00:41:29.460
<v Julian Mccullough>To give you an idea of what Rick looked like, he was about 5'7". He had been a commercial roofer for many years. He had started out in a biker gang, and I guess decided to move on from that life and became a commercial roofer. So he was very, very strong and muscly and very red. He was red everywhere from the sun and from just anger.
<v Julian Mccullough>给你一个关于里克长相的概念，他大约5英尺7英寸。他做了很多年商业屋顶工。他开始在摩托车帮里，我想他决定从那种生活中走出来，成为了一名商业屋顶工。所以他非常、非常强壮，肌肉发达，而且非常红。他全身都是红色的，来自阳光，也来自愤怒。

00:41:29.460 --> 00:42:15.500
<v Julian Mccullough>And the tension between me and Rick started immediately and built from there. See, I thought Rick was an anti-intellectual, homophobic, racist, gun-toting alcoholic. And Rick thought that I was a wimpy, arrogant, self-entitled kid whose grandparents were putting him through college. And we were both dead on.
<v Julian Mccullough>我和里克之间的紧张关系立即开始了，并从那里开始建立。你看，我认为里克是一个反智、恐同、种族主义、携带枪支的酒鬼。而里克认为我是一个懦弱、傲慢、自我感觉良好的孩子，他的祖父母让他上大学。我们俩都完全正确。

00:42:15.500 --> 00:42:56.930
<v Julian Mccullough>So finally after five years of going down there in the summers, my mom convinces me and my sister to visit her for Christmas for the first time. She had always been begging, "Hey, maybe for Christmas one year you should come down." And I didn't want to do that for a couple reasons. One, you just heard everything. Two, I had a sweet Christmas going on with my dad's parents. My grandparents in Philadelphia threw the most amazing Christmas. They threw a Thomas Kinkade painting Christmas.
<v Julian Mccullough>所以终于在夏天去那里五年后，我母亲说服我和我妹妹第一次在圣诞节去探望她。她一直都在恳求，“嘿，也许有一年圣诞节你们应该下来。”我不想那样做，原因有几个。一，你刚听到了所有事情。二，我父亲的父母那边过着很棒的圣诞节。我在费城的祖父母举办了最精彩的圣诞节。他们举办了一个像托马斯·金凯德（Thomas Kinkade）画作一样的圣诞节。

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:28.730
<v Julian Mccullough>But finally me and my sister-- she lived in San Francisco at the time, she was older --decided we would do that for my mom. And we go to Christmas. Now Rick was on his best behavior. In fact, he had a smile plastered on his face that my mom had clearly put there for him. But it's going fine. He's behaving himself, and so are we.
<v Julian Mccullough>但最终我和我妹妹——她当时住在旧金山，她年龄较大——决定我们应该为我母亲做那个。我们去过圣诞节。现在里克表现得最好。事实上，他脸上挂着我母亲显然为他放上去的微笑。但一切顺利。他表现得很好，我们也一样。

00:43:28.730 --> 00:44:10.610
<v Julian Mccullough>And we have dinner that night, and my mom cooked a great dinner. One other thing is that my mom was a vegetarian my entire life. And she also was a poet, and she read all the time, and she meditated. She did all these things until she got to Tennessee and she threw all of that out the window, because it made them uncomfortable. So she started eating meat and cooking ribs and wings and shepherds pie. And that's what we had for dinner. She also got tattoos at 47. She got a black widow spider on her hand.
<v Julian Mccullough>那天晚上我们吃晚饭，我母亲做了一顿丰盛的晚餐。另一件事是我母亲我一生中都是素食主义者。她也是一位诗人，她总是阅读，而且她冥想。她做了所有这些事情，直到她到达田纳西州，她把所有这些都抛到了窗外，因为那让他们感到不舒服。所以她开始吃肉，做排骨、翅膀和牧羊人派。这就是我们晚餐吃的。她还在47岁时纹了纹身。她在手上纹了一只黑寡妇蜘蛛。

00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:19.630
<v Julian Mccullough>[LAUGHTER]
<v Julian Mccullough>（笑声）

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:40.940
<v Ira Glass>That's the laugh of a man who that's not his mom. She also had a tattoo of an apple with a bite taken out of it on her upper thigh, which I didn't need to see to believe.
<v Ira Glass>那是那个不是他母亲的男人的笑声。她在大腿上还有一个被咬了一口的苹果纹身，我不需要看就知道是真的。

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:59.990
<v Ira Glass>And by the way, I have to give them credit. They did a great job on the house. It was decorated, lit with Christmas lights everywhere. The tree was big and decorated. And there were presents. And we were sitting down to this dinner, and my mom was obviously really excited to have us there. She was so happy, she was choking back tears.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一下，我必须给他们打分。他们在房子里做得很棒。它经过装饰，到处都点亮了圣诞灯。树很大，装饰得很漂亮。还有礼物。我们坐下来吃这顿饭，我母亲显然很高兴我们能在那里。她太高兴了，她噎住了眼泪。

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:44.710
<v Ira Glass>And Rick was drinking tequila out of a bottle to celebrate the fact that he had tequila, a whole bottle. So he was drinking Patron Silver, which if you don't know is a very unique bottle. It's a very thick, square, heavy bottle. So he's drinking that. He finishes the entire bottle of Patron Silver at dinner. And like a human, passes out.
<v Ira Glass>里克在喝瓶子里的龙舌兰酒，庆祝他有龙舌兰酒，一整瓶。所以他喝的是Patron Silver，如果你不知道，那是一个非常独特的瓶子。这是一个非常厚、正方形、沉重的瓶子。所以他在喝那个。他在晚餐时喝完了整瓶Patron Silver。像人类一样，晕过去了。

00:45:44.710 --> 00:46:01.630
<v Ira Glass>So he goes to lay down, and now it's just me and my mom and my sister. And it's beautiful. And we're having Christmas. And it's the tree and the fireplace and the music. And we're just catching up. And I'm making my mom and my sister laugh with stories about college. And it's going really nicely.
<v Ira Glass>所以他去躺下，现在只有我和我妈还有我妹妹。这很美。我们正在过圣诞节。有树、壁炉和音乐。我们只是在叙旧。我讲着关于大学的故事，让我妈和我妹妹笑。一切都进行得很好。

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:58.540
<v Ira Glass>And then the subject comes up-- I think my mom brought it up --of Rick's pride and how it's always an issue, the Southern/ Northern thing. And I said, "You know, it's just so weird for us to understand why he cares so much about that, because you know what the North thinks about the South?" And she said, "What?" And I said, "We don't. We pretty much won and we're like, bye!" Which I was very proud of at the time. I thought that was very witty. Somebody who didn't like it was Rick, who apparently had been awake enough to hear me say that.
<v Ira Glass>然后话题提出来了——我想是我妈提出来的——关于里克的自尊心，以及南方/北方的问题为什么总是一个问题。我说，“你知道，我们要理解他为什么那么在乎这一点真的很奇怪，因为你知道北方对南方怎么看吗？”她说，“怎么看？”我说，“我们不看。我们基本上赢了，我们就像，拜拜！”当时我对此感到非常自豪。我觉得那很机智。不喜欢它的人是里克，显然他醒着，听到了我说的话。

00:46:58.540 --> 00:47:01.310
<v Ira Glass>[BOTTLE ROLLING ON THE FLOOR]
<v Ira Glass>（瓶子在地板上滚动）

00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:33.190
<v Ira Glass>And that was him getting off of the bed. I've been doing comedy for nine years. That's the first time a bottle rolling on the floor was perfect for the story. "Uh-oh. He's awake! It's a Christmas miracle."
<v Ira Glass>那就是他从床上起来。我做喜剧九年了。这是第一次瓶子在地板上滚动对这个故事来说是完美的。“呃哦。他醒了！这是个圣诞奇迹。”

00:47:33.190 --> 00:48:45.200
<v Ira Glass>All of a sudden, Rick comes out of his bedroom like a bull in a rodeo. He charges across the room with just jeans on and no shirt. So he's got all his prison tats showing. He's bright red. His eyes are just as red as his skin. And I don't know if any of you have ever dealt with someone that was so drunk that they weren't there, but Rick wasn't there. And I've been struggling with how to describe what he was saying for a show like this. So let's just say I'll say what he said, and you guys can put in the F word every other word. Fair enough? He came out of the room straight at me first saying, "Get out of my house." And then to my sister, "You get out of my house." Are we doing the F thing? OK.
<v Ira Glass>突然，里克像罗迪欧里的公牛一样从卧室里出来。他冲过房间，只穿着牛仔裤，没穿上衣。所以他所有的监狱纹身都露了出来。他红得发亮。他的眼睛和他的皮肤一样红。我不知道你们中是否有人曾经和喝得烂醉以至于不在状态的人打交道，但里克不在状态。我一直在努力描述他在像这样的节目里说的话。所以让我们说，我说出他说的话，你们可以每隔一个词加入那个F词。公平吗？他从房间里出来，直接冲向我，先说，“滚出我的房子。”然后对我妹妹，“你滚出我的房子。”我们在做那个F词吗？好的。

00:48:45.200 --> 00:49:21.930
<v Ira Glass>My mom said, "Rick, please calm down and go back to bed." And I say, "Yeah." And he said-- you can add in a couple of B's and C's on this one too. He said, "I don't want these spoiled Yanks in my house. Nobody comes into my house in my country and tells me that I'm not good enough." So my mom says, "Rick, you're drunk and you're being stupid."
<v Ira Glass>我妈说，“里克，请冷静下来，回床上睡觉。”我说，“是的。”他说——你也可以在这个上面加几个B词和C词。他说，“我不想要这些被宠坏的北方佬在我的房子里。没有人能进入我的房子、我的国家，告诉我不够好。”所以我妈说，“里克，你喝醉了，你很愚蠢。”

00:49:21.930 --> 00:50:04.190
<v Ira Glass>And that's when he turned, grabbed the bottle of Patron Silver that was empty, and threw it at my mom as hard as he could. It hit her in the chest and made a sickening smack sound. And her chest immediately started to turned black. My mom bruises easily, but that's not fair either way. So my mom said to Rick, "You promised you wouldn't do this on Christmas." And that's when me and my sister realized that this was the kind of thing that was going on a lot.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时他转身，抓起那瓶空了的Patron Silver，尽可能用力地朝我妈扔去。它打在她的胸口，发出令人作呕的啪的一声。她的胸口立刻开始变黑。我妈很容易擦伤，但这无论如何都不公平。所以我妈对里克说，“你答应过你不会在圣诞节这样做的。”就在那时，我和我妹妹意识到这就是经常发生的那种事情。

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:43.940
<v Ira Glass>So then he turns to me and he says, "I will kill you tonight." And people say that a lot I think and don't mean it, but that's not what he was doing. So now me and my sister decide to cower behind the Christmas tree, which is a weird place for a Christmas tree to be. They're not used to breaking up fights. In fact, the tree was so scared, I think it unplugged itself so that Rick wouldn't notice it.
<v Ira Glass>然后他转向我，说，“我今晚杀了你。”人们经常那样说，我想，但并不是认真的，但那不是他正在做的。所以现在我和我妹妹决定躲在圣诞树后面，这是一个圣诞树待的奇怪地方。它们不习惯打架。事实上，那棵树太害怕了，我想它把自己拔掉了，这样里克就不会注意到它。

00:50:43.940 --> 00:51:13.480
<v Ira Glass>So now my mom decides that I guess we could leave. So we leave. We get in the car, and Rick is chasing us out to the driveway. And we get in a car and just start driving at 1:00AM on Christmas Eve in our pajamas with nowhere to go. We eventually find a hotel and stay there overnight. We're kind of shell-shocked. Nobody really says much.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在我妈决定我想我们可以走。所以我们走了。我们上了车，里克追着我们到车道。我们上了一辆车，只是在圣诞前夜凌晨1点开着，穿着睡衣，无处可去。我们最终找到了一个旅馆，在那里过了一夜。我们有点惊魂未定。没人说太多话。

00:51:13.480 --> 00:52:03.860
<v Ira Glass>We wake up in the morning and we go to the police station, because my mom needs to go get some things and we don't want to go there without police. Also there was a lot of presents. So we show up back at the house with the police escort, and they had turned the lights on on top of the car, which I thought was festive. And I don't know if you've ever gone into a house with a homicidal maniac and a police escort for your presents, but it's very different than coming down the stairs.
<v Ira Glass>早上我们醒来，去警察局，因为我妈需要去拿一些东西，我们不想在没有警察的情况下过去。还有很多礼物。所以我们在警察护送下回到房子，他们打开了车顶上的灯，我觉得这很有节日气氛。我不知道你是否曾经在凶手和一个警察护送的情况下为了你的礼物进过一所房子，但这和走下楼梯完全不同。

00:52:03.860 --> 00:52:28.550
<v Ira Glass>So we collected our presents and put them in the trunk and drove away. We went to a Waffle House for breakfast. And normally if you told me, hey, you were going to spend Christmas morning at a Waffle House, I would be depressed. But after that night, I didn't care that the eggs weren't locally grown or whatever.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们收集了礼物，把它们放在后备箱里开车走了。我们去一家Waffle House吃早餐。通常如果你告诉我，嘿，你要在Waffle House过圣诞早晨，我会很沮丧。但在那天晚上之后，我不在乎鸡蛋是不是本地种植的或者其他什么。

00:52:28.550 --> 00:53:15.120
<v Ira Glass>So we were sitting at breakfast, and it was then that I realized that I'd been going to Tennessee every year because I didn't want my mom to think that I'd abandoned her. I wanted her to think that in some way I was validating her choice, that I was encouraging her and her new life, even though I hated every decision she made. And it was after that Christmas Eve that I realized I had no more burden to go to Tennessee ever again. That was the best present I could have gotten.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们坐在早餐桌旁，就在那时我意识到我每年都去田纳西州是因为我不希望我妈认为我抛弃了她。我希望她认为我在某种程度上是在肯定她的选择，我在鼓励她和她的新生活，尽管我讨厌她做的每一个决定。就在那个圣诞前夜之后，我意识到我再也没有负担去田纳西州了。那是我想得到的最好的礼物。

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:21.310
<v Ira Glass>Thank you very much.
<v Ira Glass>非常感谢。

00:53:21.310 --> 00:53:32.390
<v Ira Glass>Julian McCullough. His Comedy Central Presents special can be seen on his website, julianmccoullough.com.
<v Ira Glass>朱利安·麦卡洛。他的Comedy Central Presents特别节目可以在他的网站julianmccoullough.com上看到。

00:53:32.390 --> 00:54:11.750
<v Dave Hill>One, two, three, four. [SINGING - "THE BEST CHRISTMAS SONG EVER"] It's Christmas time. And Baby, please don't come home. Not even if you want to. Because for you I've got no Christmas cheer. It's Christmas time. And, Baby, please don't come home, not even if you want to, not after what happened last year.
